    "created_at": "2026-03-25T15:33:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-03-25T15:33:59Z",
    "body": "Coming back to this now, and sorry for anything I'm muddling up because I do not find these algorithms easy to wrap my head around. 😅 \n\n> Setting a custom element registry on the inert document would make it so that, for example, custom element constructors would be called as a result of createContextualFragment.\n\n> I'm a little confused because my initial interpretation would be that fragment parsing should not have a custom element registry to keep elements from being upgraded during fragment parsing.\n\nYeah I'm wrong with my solution here. I had originally been debugging a regression on a Friday afternoon, finally found something that seemed to make sense and fix it, and reported it. But I clearly had that a bit wrong. The regression I was seeing without this is in https://wpt.live/custom-elements/builtin-coverage.html : all of the `ELEMENT: innerHTML should instantiate a customized built-in element` test cases fail, with the element not getting upgraded:\n\n<img width=\"1029\" height=\"299\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4ae7bc5f-a601-431c-b0c6-f6116131c6d3\" />\n\nAnd, I think I figured out the actual issue! And, it's already been fixed, but I missed it when I last checked for spec changes. 😆 \n\n[Adopt node](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-node-adopt), step 3.1.3.2 didn't check for element having a `null` CER before, but now does. That solves the problem. It does create some other failures, for example the two last subtests of https://wpt.fyi/results/custom-elements/registries/Element-innerHTML.html?label=experimental&label=master&aligned which Safari fails, also fail because of that null check. But that's a separate thing. I'll close this now.",
